gm-method (when a workaround papers over a symptom, fixing the true root cause makes the workaround REDUNDANT AND HARMFUL -- delete it, do not keep it as belt-and-suspenders): a per-instance instanced-LOD vanish was first patched with a force-draw wrap (append an in-frustum-but-unbucketed instance into a LOD level). Later the TRUE root cause was found (two LOD levels sharing one geometry object -> aliased index buffers). After fixing that at source, the force-draw wrap kept re-adding a near instance to LOD0 while the lib correctly drew it at LOD1 -> the tree DOUBLE-DREW across a band (the user saw 'levels overlap at the changeover instead of swapping outright'). The lesson: once you fix the root, RE-WITNESS whether each earlier workaround is still needed; a stateless single-assignment system (one bucket per instance per frame) is corrupted by ANY external append. Witness via a per-instance bucket sweep that flags BOTH zero buckets (vanish) AND >1 bucket (double-draw) across the full distance range -- the same probe proves the workaround is removable (issues=[] with it disabled) and proves the removal correct. Keeping a now-redundant correctness hack is not safety, it is a second bug.